WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.669
HTTP client resilience begins with timeouts.

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:08.008
Connection timeouts prevent a client from waiting indefinitely to
establish a connection.

3
00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:12.320
Read timeouts prevent a client from hanging while a remote service stalls.

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:18.754
Retries can help with transient failures, but they need limits, backoff,
and an understanding of idempotency.

5
00:00:18.754 --> 00:00:27.309
A retry that repeats a state-changing operation can create duplicate work,
incorrect charges, repeated notifications, or inconsistent records.

6
00:00:27.309 --> 00:00:32.443
Safe logging should make outbound behavior observable without leaking
sensitive data.

7
00:00:32.443 --> 00:00:41.135
Logs can include dependency name, operation name, status category,
duration, timeout status, retry count, correlation ID, and safe error
category.

8
00:00:41.135 --> 00:00:50.991
They should avoid tokens, credentials, authorization headers, full
sensitive URLs, private payloads, unnecessary response bodies, and raw
downstream error content.

9
00:00:50.991 --> 00:00:55.851
Redaction should apply consistently to logs, traces, alerts, and support
bundles.

10
00:00:55.851 --> 00:00:58.383
Testing should cover more than happy paths.

11
00:00:58.383 --> 00:01:16.179
Good tests exercise destination validation, TLS failure handling at a high
level, redirect policy, proxy assumptions where relevant, timeouts,
unavailable services, malformed responses, oversized responses, unexpected
content types, authentication failures, safe parsing, and logging
redaction.

12
00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:23.981
Controlled test doubles or fake endpoints can make these behaviors
reliable to verify without depending on fragile external systems.

13
00:01:23.981 --> 00:01:35.480
Monitoring should surface unusual destinations, authentication failures,
error spikes, latency changes, retry storms, certificate errors, redirect
anomalies, and unexpected response sizes.

14
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:49.374
If credentials, requests, or responses are exposed, incident response
should assess what leaked, rotate or revoke secrets when appropriate,
contain affected integrations, review logs, fix the client behavior, and
add regression tests.

15
00:01:49.374 --> 00:01:53.960
HTTP client security has to remain observable without becoming noisy or
leaky.
